==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱley-|id=incline}}
First attested in 1614. From {{bor|en|la|acclīvitās}}, from {{m|la|acclīvis||ascending}}, from {{m|la|ad}} + {{m|la|clīvus||slope}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈklɪv.ə.ti/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-acclivity.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geomorphology}} A slope or inclination of the earth, as the side of a hill, considered as ''ascending'', in opposition to ''[[declivity]]'', or ''descending''; an upward slope; [[ascent]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891
|author=w:Thomas Hardy|title=w:Tess of the d'Urbervilles|passage=she would walk {{...}} as far as to the point where the '''acclivity''' from the valley began its first steep ascent to the outer world.}}
#* {{RQ:Grey Riders|8|passage=Just below it leaned a tottering crag that would have toppled, starting an avalanche on an '''acclivity''' where no sliding mass could stop.}}

